WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.469 --> 00:00:02.760
MILO BABIC: Little bit about
iStreamPlanet, who we are.

00:00:02.760 --> 00:00:04.176
When you think
about us, you think

00:00:04.176 --> 00:00:08.290
about that company that
takes a single broadcast feed

00:00:08.290 --> 00:00:12.560
and then transforms that
for distribution for all

00:00:12.560 --> 00:00:13.650
these different devices.

00:00:13.650 --> 00:00:16.980
So if you pick up NBC Live
Sports Extra app, for example,

00:00:16.980 --> 00:00:20.280
and you're watching Premier
League or you're watching NFL

00:00:20.280 --> 00:00:23.475
or you're watching
NBA or Formula One

00:00:23.475 --> 00:00:24.850
or any number of
different sports

00:00:24.850 --> 00:00:27.050
that we support on
an ongoing basis,

00:00:27.050 --> 00:00:29.290
we're responsible for
taking that broadcast

00:00:29.290 --> 00:00:32.000
feed, conditioning,
inserting all the ads,

00:00:32.000 --> 00:00:34.940
all the closed captioning,
providing all the security,

00:00:34.940 --> 00:00:37.680
and the [INAUDIBLE] management,
encryption, and then

00:00:37.680 --> 00:00:42.309
conditioning that stream, once
again, especially for different

00:00:42.309 --> 00:00:44.600
sets of devices by creating
different playlists for all

00:00:44.600 --> 00:00:46.380
those different devices.

00:00:46.380 --> 00:00:49.730
And we do this on a
daily basis for something

00:00:49.730 --> 00:00:53.950
close to about 200 linear
channels that we operate today

00:00:53.950 --> 00:00:58.150
and approximately 250
different live events.

00:00:58.150 --> 00:01:02.160
Anything from all these
premium sports or media

00:01:02.160 --> 00:01:04.094
brands that you see
here on the screen.

00:01:06.564 --> 00:01:07.980
So what's new in
the market space,

00:01:07.980 --> 00:01:10.690
and why are we so excited to
be collaborating with Google?

00:01:10.690 --> 00:01:13.200
And really, what
led us to Google?

00:01:13.200 --> 00:01:16.880
I think it's hard not to notice
that all these announcements--

00:01:16.880 --> 00:01:19.990
new exciting announcements--
about TV finally getting

00:01:19.990 --> 00:01:22.320
transformed after
all these decades.

00:01:22.320 --> 00:01:25.760
Saying the same stuff--
setup box, remote control,

00:01:25.760 --> 00:01:30.160
very, very, very slow user
guide, and limited number

00:01:30.160 --> 00:01:32.840
of channels or certain bundles.

00:01:32.840 --> 00:01:34.370
And then all of
sudden what happens

00:01:34.370 --> 00:01:38.440
is HBO announces they're
going over the top-- you can

00:01:38.440 --> 00:01:42.530
subscribe, and you can watch all
the great content HBO produces

00:01:42.530 --> 00:01:45.360
and creates for us
live and on demand

00:01:45.360 --> 00:01:47.927
and you don't need a
cable subscription.

00:01:47.927 --> 00:01:49.510
And then many others
followed to this.

00:01:49.510 --> 00:01:52.140
Sling TV, which is
a product of a dish,

00:01:52.140 --> 00:01:54.570
creates what we call a
skinny bundle-- unbundles

00:01:54.570 --> 00:01:56.110
a bunch of channels.

00:01:56.110 --> 00:01:59.980
Creates a pretty compelling
bundle that probably 80% of us

00:01:59.980 --> 00:02:02.230
use on a daily basis and
makes it available to us

00:02:02.230 --> 00:02:04.360
over the top on any device.

00:02:04.360 --> 00:02:06.130
And many others.

00:02:06.130 --> 00:02:09.520
Now, as exciting as
that is, there really

00:02:09.520 --> 00:02:11.494
are three big challenges.

00:02:11.494 --> 00:02:13.410
And instead of talking
about these challenges,

00:02:13.410 --> 00:02:15.020
I'm going to tell
you a little story.

00:02:15.020 --> 00:02:18.000
It is 2010 and
we're getting ready.

00:02:18.000 --> 00:02:20.680
It's the end of
January 2010, and we're

00:02:20.680 --> 00:02:23.920
getting ready to stream
the Vancouver Olympics

00:02:23.920 --> 00:02:26.070
for the first time in HD.

00:02:26.070 --> 00:02:30.290
And we had 28 different live
sources coming all the way

00:02:30.290 --> 00:02:33.717
from Vancouver, NBC,
and the Netherlands

00:02:33.717 --> 00:02:36.050
because we had three different
broadcasters that we were

00:02:36.050 --> 00:02:38.630
working with, as well as
the Canadian folks-- we

00:02:38.630 --> 00:02:40.340
had six channels from Canada.

00:02:40.340 --> 00:02:43.260
And I remember going back home--
it's about 3:00 in the morning

00:02:43.260 --> 00:02:46.160
after one of those long, long,
long, long days-- turning

00:02:46.160 --> 00:02:49.070
on my Cox cable and there
was only 16 HD channels

00:02:49.070 --> 00:02:49.900
at the time.

00:02:49.900 --> 00:02:52.180
We were running 28
of these channels

00:02:52.180 --> 00:02:55.810
for 18 days and about
3,000 live events

00:02:55.810 --> 00:02:59.049
covering every single
second of the games.

00:02:59.049 --> 00:03:01.340
But really the point I'm
trying to make is about a week

00:03:01.340 --> 00:03:02.840
prior to the Olympics,
we get a call

00:03:02.840 --> 00:03:04.820
from two more broadcasters.

00:03:04.820 --> 00:03:09.230
And when they told me, they said
Milo, we need 10 more sources.

00:03:09.230 --> 00:03:10.660
And how much is
it going to cost?

00:03:10.660 --> 00:03:12.067
And I said, I can't do it.

00:03:12.067 --> 00:03:13.900
They said, what do you
mean you can't do it?

00:03:13.900 --> 00:03:16.150
We'll pay you $100,000 for
each one of these sources.

00:03:16.150 --> 00:03:19.720
I said, if you pay me
$1 billion I can't do it

00:03:19.720 --> 00:03:23.240
unless I take away from
hardware, software, and all

00:03:23.240 --> 00:03:25.990
the cable and everything
else that we have allocated

00:03:25.990 --> 00:03:27.710
for our current customers.

00:03:27.710 --> 00:03:30.030
It would take me
approximately six weeks

00:03:30.030 --> 00:03:31.090
to procure the equipment.

00:03:31.090 --> 00:03:32.548
It would take me
another four weeks

00:03:32.548 --> 00:03:36.590
to get everything cabled up,
powered up, and turned on.

00:03:36.590 --> 00:03:38.720
And then probably another
two to three weeks

00:03:38.720 --> 00:03:41.610
to get everything integrated
with end-to-end workflow.

00:03:41.610 --> 00:03:43.840
And in the meantime, the
Olympics will be long gone.

00:03:43.840 --> 00:03:47.270
So needless to say,
we did not end up

00:03:47.270 --> 00:03:49.910
providing services for these
two additional broadcasters.

00:03:49.910 --> 00:03:53.850
But it left an impactful
thought in my mind

00:03:53.850 --> 00:03:56.754
that we didn't have scale.

00:03:56.754 --> 00:03:58.670
Not only did we not have
scale, but we did not

00:03:58.670 --> 00:04:02.476
have ability to elastically
scale based on demand.

00:04:02.476 --> 00:04:05.740
The complexity of workflow
was too high, too long.

00:04:05.740 --> 00:04:08.334
We took too long to
provision things.

00:04:08.334 --> 00:04:09.750
And all these
different hardware--

00:04:09.750 --> 00:04:11.740
all these different
appliances that it

00:04:11.740 --> 00:04:14.540
takes to organize
this premium content

00:04:14.540 --> 00:04:17.321
and delivery across
these different devices--

00:04:17.321 --> 00:04:18.279
they're very expensive.

00:04:18.279 --> 00:04:21.000
Each one of these boxes
are $20,000 to $30,000

00:04:21.000 --> 00:04:23.000
and you've got to integrate
them and the cabling

00:04:23.000 --> 00:04:24.310
and time and everything else.

00:04:24.310 --> 00:04:27.320
Obviously, that
was a huge problem.

00:04:27.320 --> 00:04:30.670
So what we did is we set
on to solve this problem.

00:04:30.670 --> 00:04:35.180
Part of the vision was how do we
move all this complex workflow

00:04:35.180 --> 00:04:37.556
all inside the software
so we don't have

00:04:37.556 --> 00:04:39.430
three or four different
appliances performing

00:04:39.430 --> 00:04:42.060
different tasks, we don't
have an enterprise bus

00:04:42.060 --> 00:04:45.600
that tries to connect all
these different systems?

00:04:45.600 --> 00:04:49.460
How do we lower the
cost of doing all this?

00:04:49.460 --> 00:04:51.510
And how do we scale dynamically?

00:04:51.510 --> 00:04:53.830
So if it's Olympics
time and we need

00:04:53.830 --> 00:04:55.680
to go-- for example,
for Rio, we're

00:04:55.680 --> 00:04:58.595
going to have something
close to 100 different feeds.

00:04:58.595 --> 00:05:02.240
Now, on a regular day, our
customer, NBC, for example,

00:05:02.240 --> 00:05:04.850
uses something like
16 to 20 feeds.

00:05:04.850 --> 00:05:06.260
But during those
18 days, they're

00:05:06.260 --> 00:05:08.130
going to need an
additional 100 feeds.

00:05:08.130 --> 00:05:11.210
How do we turn them
up, spin them up?

00:05:11.210 --> 00:05:14.460
They operate for about 18 days--
process every single second

00:05:14.460 --> 00:05:15.260
of the games.

00:05:15.260 --> 00:05:17.160
And then on day
19, they go down.

00:05:17.160 --> 00:05:18.390
They disappear.

00:05:18.390 --> 00:05:20.870
And best of all, the customer
only pays for those 18 days

00:05:20.870 --> 00:05:22.400
that they used.

00:05:22.400 --> 00:05:24.190
So what we created
we call Aventus.

00:05:24.190 --> 00:05:25.080
It's our platform.

00:05:25.080 --> 00:05:26.470
It's our product.

00:05:26.470 --> 00:05:29.620
It's the world's first
fully-distributed product

00:05:29.620 --> 00:05:34.930
built 100% for a cloud--
solely for a cloud.

00:05:34.930 --> 00:05:37.350
The distributed
architecture allows

00:05:37.350 --> 00:05:40.780
us to acquire schedule,
acquire content, process

00:05:40.780 --> 00:05:43.700
all the content, all the
bits, insert the ads,

00:05:43.700 --> 00:05:45.960
process all the
closed captioning.

00:05:45.960 --> 00:05:48.170
In case there's
any issues with any

00:05:48.170 --> 00:05:49.870
of the parts of our
workflow, our system

00:05:49.870 --> 00:05:51.864
will automatically detect that.

00:05:51.864 --> 00:05:53.530
It will give you the
data in real time--

00:05:53.530 --> 00:05:54.730
what's happening
from a moment we

00:05:54.730 --> 00:05:56.280
get the content to
the moment content

00:05:56.280 --> 00:05:58.166
gets delivered to the end users.

00:05:58.166 --> 00:05:59.540
And then if there's
any problems,

00:05:59.540 --> 00:06:01.690
we're going to use all of
this wonderful big data

00:06:01.690 --> 00:06:05.380
that we gather and solve
the problems on the fly.

00:06:05.380 --> 00:06:07.640
And that's what we
do today with Aventus

00:06:07.640 --> 00:06:10.860
and that's what we
use for our customers.

00:06:10.860 --> 00:06:13.360
Now, why was Google
interesting to us?

00:06:13.360 --> 00:06:15.690
I think Chris from
[INAUDIBLE] and gentleman

00:06:15.690 --> 00:06:18.590
from [INAUDIBLE] did a
great job talking about I/O

00:06:18.590 --> 00:06:20.210
and all these sorts of things.

00:06:20.210 --> 00:06:24.240
So I'm only going to
focus on the networking.

00:06:24.240 --> 00:06:28.170
About nine months
ago during this show

00:06:28.170 --> 00:06:29.730
was called
International Broadcast

00:06:29.730 --> 00:06:32.440
Conference in
Amsterdam, we probably

00:06:32.440 --> 00:06:34.840
met with about 40 to
50 different customers.

00:06:34.840 --> 00:06:37.482
And the messaging was very
loud and clear to us--

00:06:37.482 --> 00:06:38.940
how do we take
advantage of Aventus

00:06:38.940 --> 00:06:42.010
and all these great products
that you guys have built?

00:06:42.010 --> 00:06:43.650
But we need it in Australia.

00:06:43.650 --> 00:06:44.800
We need it in Singapore.

00:06:44.800 --> 00:06:46.140
We need it in Japan.

00:06:46.140 --> 00:06:48.820
We need it in Frankfurt,
all over in Europe,

00:06:48.820 --> 00:06:49.650
and Latin America.

00:06:49.650 --> 00:06:51.358
The Olympics are coming
to Latin America.

00:06:51.358 --> 00:06:53.770
How do we take advantage
of all these capabilities

00:06:53.770 --> 00:06:55.200
around the world?

00:06:55.200 --> 00:06:57.310
And then lastly,
how do we do this

00:06:57.310 --> 00:06:59.900
without spending a
vast amount of money,

00:06:59.900 --> 00:07:04.530
egressing and ingesting his
content into the network?

00:07:04.530 --> 00:07:06.500
This is a very,
very big problem.

00:07:06.500 --> 00:07:09.629
So when we open up these
discussions with Google,

00:07:09.629 --> 00:07:12.170
it's great to know that search
and advertising and everything

00:07:12.170 --> 00:07:14.430
else runs on this platform,
which we can attest to it.

00:07:14.430 --> 00:07:16.090
In fact, I'm going to
break this pattern.

00:07:16.090 --> 00:07:17.715
I'm actually going
to show you a demo--

00:07:17.715 --> 00:07:19.850
the actual demo of the product.

00:07:19.850 --> 00:07:22.140
But it was really,
really cool knowing

00:07:22.140 --> 00:07:24.927
that we can ingest gigabits
of gigabits of content--

00:07:24.927 --> 00:07:27.260
I'm talking about hundreds
and hundreds of channels that

00:07:27.260 --> 00:07:30.940
are live linear events--
into the Google Cloud

00:07:30.940 --> 00:07:33.650
without actually
paying any extra cost

00:07:33.650 --> 00:07:37.110
if we're inter-carrier partners,
which was of huge value

00:07:37.110 --> 00:07:39.810
to us and our customers.

00:07:39.810 --> 00:07:43.950
So today, we run a number
of different channels.

00:07:43.950 --> 00:07:47.800
We're actually in
a beta mode today.

00:07:47.800 --> 00:07:50.430
In about 30 days, we're
going to be in production.

00:07:50.430 --> 00:07:53.080
We're looking to launch
just over 500 channels

00:07:53.080 --> 00:07:56.930
over the next 12 months and do
about 3,000 live events a month

00:07:56.930 --> 00:07:59.570
on average through a
Google Cloud Platform.

00:07:59.570 --> 00:08:01.830
But enough talking about this.

00:08:01.830 --> 00:08:03.839
Let's go into some
demos and some fun stuff

00:08:03.839 --> 00:08:05.630
here and actually show
you some video going

00:08:05.630 --> 00:08:08.547
through Google Cloud Platform.

00:08:08.547 --> 00:08:11.130
So I'm going to swap this thing
out, if that's OK, real quick.

00:08:17.840 --> 00:08:21.720
MODERATOR: You've gotta love
it when the CEO gives the demo.

00:08:21.720 --> 00:08:24.716
MILO BABIC: That's
what it's all about.

00:08:24.716 --> 00:08:27.760
You've got to show
people what we're doing

00:08:27.760 --> 00:08:30.280
and why we're excited by Google.

00:08:30.280 --> 00:08:31.400
Let's see, there we go.

00:08:41.539 --> 00:08:43.450
So what you are
looking at right now,

00:08:43.450 --> 00:08:45.140
this is our very,
very simple interface.

00:08:45.140 --> 00:08:47.200
I mean, we stole the
idea from you guys.

00:08:47.200 --> 00:08:48.660
Google search box.

00:08:48.660 --> 00:08:50.480
There's just one
box, very simple.

00:08:50.480 --> 00:08:52.870
There's no 30 menus
and sub-menus.

00:08:52.870 --> 00:08:56.180
So I'm going to create,
actually, a new channel.

00:08:56.180 --> 00:09:00.040
I'm going to call
this a GCP demo.

00:09:00.040 --> 00:09:02.680
And sources, we have
various sources.

00:09:02.680 --> 00:09:06.530
I'm going to pick the
NBC sports network.

00:09:06.530 --> 00:09:08.840
Sports is always more
fun than the news.

00:09:08.840 --> 00:09:10.260
Out of our cloud video router.

00:09:10.260 --> 00:09:12.964
So we also have a capability
to route all these video feeds,

00:09:12.964 --> 00:09:14.880
and then through our
cloud video router, which

00:09:14.880 --> 00:09:18.060
is a part of our product, we can
send this to any Google Cloud

00:09:18.060 --> 00:09:21.630
locations around the world.

00:09:21.630 --> 00:09:24.990
I'm going to leave
it at default preset.

00:09:24.990 --> 00:09:28.460
The language, I'll have
Aventus reconfigure all that

00:09:28.460 --> 00:09:30.340
automatically for me.

00:09:30.340 --> 00:09:34.520
And I'm going to enable--
so instead of typing

00:09:34.520 --> 00:09:38.420
all this data, I have NAB demo.

00:09:38.420 --> 00:09:42.310
And I'm going to click
on Start channel.

00:09:42.310 --> 00:09:46.150
So now what happens
here is-- what I've done

00:09:46.150 --> 00:09:49.190
is essentially I've
created a JSON payload.

00:09:49.190 --> 00:09:51.760
This says, go ahead
and find this source.

00:09:51.760 --> 00:09:54.600
Subscribe to this
particular source.

00:09:54.600 --> 00:09:57.590
Do this particular encoding
configuration on it.

00:09:57.590 --> 00:09:59.770
Dynamically determine what
kind of source comes in,

00:09:59.770 --> 00:10:01.728
what kind of outputs can
we create out of that,

00:10:01.728 --> 00:10:04.820
and then create the HLS
package and HLS format.

00:10:04.820 --> 00:10:08.634
And that JSON file gets sent
out to our cloud orchestration

00:10:08.634 --> 00:10:10.300
layer, and then our
orchestration layer,

00:10:10.300 --> 00:10:13.560
what our orchestration layer
does, it unpacks that and says,

00:10:13.560 --> 00:10:17.330
OK, well, now I have
this kind of encoding,

00:10:17.330 --> 00:10:19.910
I have these different bit
rates, this kind of packaging,

00:10:19.910 --> 00:10:21.119
this is where my source is.

00:10:21.119 --> 00:10:23.160
I need this much computational
power from Google.

00:10:23.160 --> 00:10:24.380
Well, it's too fast.

00:10:24.380 --> 00:10:25.630
I can't even finish my speech.

00:10:25.630 --> 00:10:26.130
I'm sorry.

00:10:28.480 --> 00:10:31.100
Your cloud works really well.

00:10:31.100 --> 00:10:33.950
So what it does, it says, I
essentially need this much CPU,

00:10:33.950 --> 00:10:38.450
I need this much storage, I need
this much egress bandwidth out.

00:10:38.450 --> 00:10:40.610
In this case, through
Akamai, we have not

00:10:40.610 --> 00:10:41.950
done a work yet [INAUDIBLE].

00:10:41.950 --> 00:10:43.390
Apologies for that.

00:10:43.390 --> 00:10:46.010
We should probably
talk afterwards.

00:10:46.010 --> 00:10:49.230
But we're publishing all
this content to Akamai,

00:10:49.230 --> 00:10:52.430
and it essentially creates
this channel on the fly.

00:10:52.430 --> 00:10:56.280
And what you're seeing
here is actually a preview.

00:10:56.280 --> 00:10:59.650
So we're generating a preview
about every five seconds.

00:10:59.650 --> 00:11:01.454
We're generating a
preview of what's

00:11:01.454 --> 00:11:03.120
coming into the Google
Cloud environment

00:11:03.120 --> 00:11:05.660
where we're actually
processing in real time.

00:11:05.660 --> 00:11:07.390
And what you can see
here, we're creating

00:11:07.390 --> 00:11:08.800
seven different layers.

00:11:08.800 --> 00:11:13.100
It's 720p output, 64k audio.

00:11:13.100 --> 00:11:15.070
By the way, everything
that we run in Aventus

00:11:15.070 --> 00:11:16.450
is based on APIs.

00:11:16.450 --> 00:11:20.090
Even our own UI runs and
calls the same sets of APIs

00:11:20.090 --> 00:11:22.640
to create this
type of experience.

00:11:22.640 --> 00:11:27.525
And then I can perform all
sorts of slight insertions,

00:11:27.525 --> 00:11:29.940
add insertion, do
all the blackouts.

00:11:29.940 --> 00:11:31.550
I can check my channel health.

00:11:31.550 --> 00:11:33.300
So we'll collect all
the data in real time

00:11:33.300 --> 00:11:34.540
and expose that to you.

00:11:34.540 --> 00:11:36.740
Everything from the moment
we ingest the content

00:11:36.740 --> 00:11:38.680
to the decode, encode,
packaging, encryption,

00:11:38.680 --> 00:11:40.790
publishing, ad
insertion-- we will

00:11:40.790 --> 00:11:42.570
track everything in
real time and use

00:11:42.570 --> 00:11:44.070
that data to fix problems.

00:11:44.070 --> 00:11:45.800
If there are any
issues, and it seems

00:11:45.800 --> 00:11:47.508
like there was one
issue with something--

00:11:47.508 --> 00:11:49.920
I thought I saw a little
blip on ingest-- alerts

00:11:49.920 --> 00:11:51.760
will pop up automatically
in real time.

00:11:51.760 --> 00:11:55.450
